@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: why a `kind: sandbox` Kit can declare NO credential and still reach a prompt, and the rung that catches it
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go (agentCredentials, Spec, storedSecretNames)
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/own_agent_test.go
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/provider_gate_test.go
' covers: e2e/ladder_test.go
' covers: internal/sbx/kit.go (KitCredential, KitCredAPIKey, KitCredInject)
' The mixin-era account this SUPERSEDES for the own-agent path:
'   _spec/internal/sbx/credential-path.puml — still correct for a mixin, and its
'   "sbx.Kit has no Credentials field, deliberately" no longer holds here.
' The capability record that reintroduced the block: _spec/_experiments/sbx-kit-capabilities.puml (E2)
' The domain grammar the allowlist speaks: _spec/internal/sbx/kit-domain-form.puml
' The ladder this adds a rung to: _spec/_paradigms/capability-ladder.puml
' Notes here are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── THE INCIDENT, AND WHAT IT TURNED OUT TO BE ─────────────────────────────
' Session proveo-1788935973-18915, target cecli, 2026-09-08T23:39. The session
' started, reached a prompt, and held. Then `/model xai` froze the TUI with a
' 403 on api.x.ai.
'
' THE 403 WAS xAI's, AND IT WAS BILLING. Read from inside a live sandbox:
'
'     {"code":"permission-denied","error":"Your team <id> has either used all
'      available credits or reached its monthly spending limit."}
'
' Nothing in proveo was broken. This file is NOT the cause of that session, and
' the first three readings of it were all wrong: the cecli Dockerfile, then the
' credential gap below, then Cloudflare fingerprinting the MITM leg. Each was
' abandoned by evidence that was already on disk.
'
' kit-domain-form.puml predicted the trap in its own words — "the agent then
' reports the PROVIDER's refusal, which reads to an operator as an egress
' denial. That misreading is the whole defect." It happened again, to a reader
' who had just finished quoting it. THAT is the finding worth keeping: a 403
' from a provider is indistinguishable from a 403 from the proxy until somebody
' reads the BODY, and no rung, log line or posture row was reading it.
'
' ── WHAT THE RUN ACTUALLY PROVED, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE ────────────────────
' The primed path works end to end, and this is the first time any of it has
' been measured against a live sandbox rather than rendered:
'
'   sbx secret ls        holds BOTH `XAI_API_KEY` and the service `xai`
'   sbx policy log       api.x.ai:443 ALLOWED, proxy `forward`, count 1 — no
'                        blocked section at all, and `sbx policy ls` carries no
'                        deny rule anywhere
'   in the agent         XAI_API_KEY = "proxy-managed" — the sentinel, never
'                        the key, so the credential boundary held
'   CONNECT reply        HTTP/1.0 200 OK, ALPN unnegotiated — the MITM leg,
'                        exactly the signature kit-domain-form.puml describes
'   the control          `Authorization: Bearer xai-junk` from inside the
'                        sandbox came back with the ACCOUNT's own billing
'                        error, which can only happen if the proxy REPLACED
'                        the header
'
' That last line answers a question sbx-kit-capabilities.puml still lists as
' unproven: whether the proxy can inject a WORKING value. For a stored API-key
' secret it can, and the junk-key control is the proof. (An OAuth-backed
' service is still untested.)
'
' ── WHAT THE KIT ACTUALLY CARRIED (rendered, not inferred) ─────────────────
' `Spec` was run for that exact key set in both states of sbx's secret store:
'
'   sbx secret ls holds nothing under a service name  -> NO credentials: block
'   sbx secret ls holds anthropic openai xai google   -> four proxyManaged
'                                                        entries, inject rules
'                                                        per host
'
' The allowlist is identical either way: x.ai, *.x.ai, anthropic.com,
' *.anthropic.com, openai.com, *.openai.com, generativelanguage.googleapis.com.
' So on an un-primed host REACH is granted for a provider whose credential is
' never declared. THAT is the defect this file names — and it is LATENT, not
' the incident: the host above held the service secret, so the credential was
' declared and injected. A host that has never run `sbx secret set <service>`
' has the asymmetry and no way to see it.
'
' ── WHY THE STORE NEVER SATISFIES THE GATE BY ITSELF ───────────────────────
' `agentCredentials` declares a service only when `sbx secret ls` already holds
' one under the SERVICE name, and the vendor reference is explicit about why:
' "service — Credential identifier, matched against the value stored with
' `sbx secret set`. Lowercase kebab-case."
'
' proveo stores under the ENV VAR name — `sbx secret set --force XAI_API_KEY` —
' and refuses to store under the service name ON PURPOSE. That refusal is
' pinned: TestSpecNeverStoresAServiceNamedSecret, whose message records the
' measurement behind it ("overwriting whatever the operator has there —
' measured doing exactly that to an OAuth login").
'
' So the two names never meet, and nothing in a normal run makes them meet.
' `sbx secret set xai` is an OPERATOR action proveo does not and should not
' perform. The default state of any host proveo has not been hand-primed on is
' therefore: allowlist yes, credential no.
'
' The host that produced the session above HAD been primed — `sbx secret ls`
' showed `XAI_API_KEY` and `xai` side by side, and sbx-kit-capabilities.puml
' records the credprobe run that put the second one there. Which is the hazard
' in its own right: the property under test was true only because an earlier
' EXPERIMENT had left a secret behind, in a store that is host-wide and
' outlives every run. Nothing tells an operator which of their hosts is primed.
'
' ── AND THE VARIABLE IS NOT UNSET, WHICH IS WHAT HIDES IT ──────────────────
' credential-path.puml settled D1 against sbx v0.39.0: sbx does NOT export
' service secrets as environment variables. With no credentials: block, the
' agent should therefore hold XAI_API_KEY UNSET, and an unset key is a quiet
' no-op — cecli skips a provider it has no key for.
'
' On the primed host it was the SENTINEL, measured: `XAI_API_KEY` read back as
' "proxy-managed" inside the live sandbox. The declaration resolved, so D1's
' rule never applied.
'
' But a second path exists and would have won if it had not. The same run
' warned about it, in its own output:
'
'     .env is inside the sandbox workspace and holds a provider key — the
'     agent's prelude sources it with `set -a`, so the key becomes agent
'     environment whatever egress tier you picked
'
' The workspace `.env` is a SECOND credential path, already recorded in
' credential-path.puml. On an un-primed host it is the ONLY one that delivers,
' and it delivers the REAL key to the agent — the exact property proxyManaged
' exists to prevent. The two paths cannot see each other, so which one is
' authenticating a run is not visible from either end.
'
' ── THE 403 VOCABULARY, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED ───────────────────────
' api.x.ai was probed with every credential shape available:
'
'     no Authorization header    401  unauthenticated:no-credentials
'     "Bearer" with no value     401  unauthenticated:bad-credentials
'     "Bearer proxy-managed"     400  Incorrect API key provided.
'     a well-formed fake key     400  Incorrect API key provided.
'     x-api-key instead          400  Incorrect API key provided.
'     two Authorization headers  400  Cloudflare, HTML
'     A KEY WHOSE TEAM IS DRY    403  permission-denied, credits/limit
'
' So 403 is NOT xAI's answer to a bad credential — every wrong-credential shape
' is 400 or 401 — and that asymmetry is what made "403" read as an egress
' denial. It IS xAI's answer to an ENTITLEMENT problem, which is the one class
' nobody was looking for.
'
' THE RULE THIS LEAVES: a 403 is unattributable without its body. proveo's
' marker lists must key on the WORDING, not the status. `authFailures` already
' carried Anthropic's "Credit balance is too low"; xAI phrases the same state
' as "used all available credits or reached its monthly spending limit", and it
' was recognised by nothing.
'
' STILL OPEN, and unrelated to the incident above:
'   WHETHER A BARE HOST NEEDS :443. docker/sbx-releases#319 shows an agent's own
'     domain blocked until allowed as "opencode.ai:443,*.opencode.ai:443", and
'     #140/#189 report kit allow rules not taking effect at all. It did not
'     matter here — `x.ai` matched, and the policy log says so — so the bare
'     form is now measured working on at least one host.
'   WHETHER `*.x.ai` IS ACCEPTED IN inject[].domain. Narrowed, not closed: the
'     proxy DID inject on api.x.ai with only `x.ai` and `*.x.ai` declared, so
'     one of the two matched. Which one is still unmeasured.
'
' ── WHAT cecli CONTRIBUTES, AND IT IS ONLY VISIBILITY ──────────────────────
' Measured against cecli 1.4.1 directly, with the completion path executed:
'   ONE call. `/model` completion walks the 63 slugs in
'     cecli/resources/providers.json and probes each whose api_key_env is set.
'     Of this run's keys only XAI_API_KEY appears there — anthropic, openai and
'     google are served by cecli's own static catalogue — so the completer makes
'     exactly one request: GET https://api.x.ai/v1/models, timeout 10s.
'   NEVER NEGATIVELY CACHED. `_update_cache` leaves the entry None on failure
'     and `_ensure_content` refetches whenever it is falsy, so it repeats on
'     every keystroke.
'   PRINTED OVER THE PROMPT. The failure is a bare `print()` from a
'     ThreadedCompleter worker while prompt_toolkit owns the screen.
'   NO OFFLINE FALLBACK. cecli's static catalogue holds ZERO xai/grok entries,
'     so `/model xai` offers 2 candidates when the probe answers 200 and 0 when
'     it answers 403. The menu can never populate.
' None of that is proveo's to fix, and none of it is the cause. A different
' agent on the same Kit fails differently and just as quietly.
'
' ── WHY THE LADDER SAID NOTHING, WHICHEVER THE CAUSE ───────────────────────
' Every rung asserts one property: the session reached a prompt and stayed
' alive for the hold window. This session did both. The ladder would have
' reported PASS for every rung, including the one that carries the Kit — and it
' would have said PASS for a dry account, for an un-primed credential, and for
' a malformed allowlist alike, because none of those stop a session STARTING.
'
' The gap is not a missing rung. It is that no rung ever USES the agent. A
' credential that is never spent and a domain that is never fetched cannot fail
' a test that only watches for a prompt.
'
' THE FIX IS ONE PARAMETER. `holdSbxSession` takes a prompt string:
'   ""            today's behaviour, exactly — reach a prompt, hold, assert
'                 nothing about what the agent can do
'   any string    typed into the PTY once the prompt is reached, then the render
'                 is scanned for the refusals below for the rest of the hold
'
' PROVEO_LADDER_PROMPT carries it, so a climb stays a climb by default and
' becomes a first-use test when an operator names one. `/model xai` is the
' string that reproduces this incident; `PROVEO_LADDER_PROMPT` is deliberately
' free text, because the next incident will need a different one.
'
' ── THE MARKERS, AND WHY EACH EARNS ITS PLACE ──────────────────────────────
'   Blocked by network policy    the three wordings sandboxd's own docs give
'   Blocked by org policy        a block, one per rule origin: default policy,
'   Blocked by local rule for    org policy, and a developer-added local deny
'   no matching allow rule       the detail line a default-deny block carries
'   403 Client Error             what raise_for_status renders in any Python
'                                agent — and it is UNATTRIBUTABLE on its own:
'                                measured as both a proxy block AND xAI's
'                                out-of-credits answer
'   Failed to fetch              cecli's model-list probe, the render measured
'   Incorrect API key provided   the sentinel reached the provider UNREPLACED,
'                                which is the injection failure, not a block
'   used all available credits   xAI's entitlement refusal, which arrives as a
'   monthly spending limit       403 and reads as an egress denial — the one
'                                the incident above actually was
' A marker list is a liability if it grows by guess, so each line names the
' thing that produced it. Two are load-bearing for opposite reasons:
' `Incorrect API key provided` is the only render separating "the proxy blocked
' me" from "the proxy let me through WITHOUT injecting"; the credit wordings are
' the only ones separating either of those from "the account is out of money".
' All three arrive as a 4xx and all three used to read the same.
'
' The credit wording also belongs in `authFailures` (e2e/idle_test.go), which
' already carried Anthropic's "Credit balance is too low" and recognised no
' other vendor's phrasing for the same state. There it is a SKIP, not a FAIL:
' an empty account makes every rung above it untestable and is nobody's bug.
title kind: sandbox credentials — a 403 nobody could attribute,\nand the rung that spends the credential

package "internal/backend/sandbox" as PKG <<host>> {
  component "Spec\nbuilds allow from in.Detected" as SPEC <<app>>
  component "agentCredentials\ngated on stored[SERVICE]" as GATE <<app>>
}

database "sbx secret store\nHOST-WIDE" as STORE <<db>>

package "the Kit proveo writes" as KIT <<host>> {
  component "permissions.network.allow\n""x.ai"", ""*.x.ai""" as ALLOW <<app>>
  component "credentials[]\nproxyManaged when primed,\nEMPTY when not" as CREDS <<error>>
}

component "workspace .env\nsourced with ""set -a""\nthe path that wins when unprimed" as DOTENV <<error>>
component "the agent\n""XAI_API_KEY"" = ""proxy-managed""" as AGENT <<app>>
cloud "sbx proxy" as PROXY <<cloud>>
component "**403** on ""api.x.ai""\nread as an egress denial" as BUG <<error>>
component "e2e ladder\nPROVEO_LADDER_PROMPT" as LADDER <<app>>

SPEC ARROW_MAIN ALLOW : reach, unconditionally
SPEC ARROW_MAIN GATE
STORE ARROW_OPTIONAL GATE : ""xai"" present -> declared;\nabsent -> silently skipped
GATE ARROW_MAIN CREDS
CREDS ARROW_MAIN AGENT : sentinel only
DOTENV ARROW_ERROR AGENT : the REAL key, when nothing\nwas primed
AGENT ARROW_CLOUD PROXY : proxy REPLACES the header\n(measured: a junk bearer still\nreached the account)
PROXY ARROW_CLOUD BUG : xAI: out of credits
BUG ARROW_QUEUE LADDER : the marker that fails the rung
LADDER ARROW_MAIN AGENT : types the prompt, scans the render

note bottom of CREDS
  **The two names never meet.** sbx resolves
  ""service"" against ""sbx secret set xai"";
  proveo stores ""XAI_API_KEY"" and refuses the
  service name on purpose. So the default is
  allowlist yes, credential no.
end note

note top of ALLOW
  **Reach is granted before the gate runs.**
  ""allow"" comes from ""in.Detected"", the
  credential from ""stored[]"" — different inputs,
  one direction. A host can be reachable with
  nothing to authenticate it.
end note

note bottom of DOTENV
  **A second path, unaware of the first.** On a
  primed host the proxy injects and this file is
  redundant; on an un-primed one it is the only
  thing that delivers, and it hands the agent the
  REAL key. Neither end can see which one ran.
end note

note bottom of BUG
  **A 403 needs its body to be attributable.**
  Measured at xAI: 401 for no or bad credentials,
  400 for a wrong key, and 403 ONLY for an
  account out of credits. Read as a block by
  three successive readings; it never was one.
end note

note right of LADDER
  **An empty prompt is today's ladder.** Reach a
  prompt, hold, assert nothing. Any string is
  typed in and the render scanned — a dry
  account, an un-primed credential and a bad
  allowlist all pass a start-up-only rung.
end note

legend bottom
  **What this file settles**
  ==
  The session that prompted it was **xAI answering 403 because the team was out of
  credits** — read from inside a live sandbox. proveo's egress and credential path
  worked end to end, and the run PROVED it: ""x.ai"" matched, the leg was allowed and
  forwarded, the agent held only ""proxy-managed"", and a junk bearer sent from inside
  still reached the account — so the proxy replaces the header.
  --
  **A 403 is unattributable without its body.** At xAI, 401 means no or bad credentials
  and 400 means a wrong key; 403 means an entitlement problem alone. Three successive
  readings of this incident called it a block. kit-domain-form.puml had already written
  down that exact trap, and the trap caught its own reader.
  ==
  **The latent defect stands, separately**: on a host nobody ran ""sbx secret set <service>""
  on, the Kit grants reach and declares no credential, and the workspace "".env"" then hands
  the agent the REAL key — the property ""proxyManaged"" exists to prevent. Nothing tells an
  operator which of their hosts is primed, and this one was primed by an old experiment.
  --
  **The ladder could not have caught any of it**: every rung asserts 'reached a prompt and
  held'. ""PROVEO_LADDER_PROMPT"" makes a rung spend the credential and read the render.
  ==
  **Still open**: whether ""x.ai"" or ""*.x.ai"" is the pattern that matched ""inject[].domain"";
  whether an OAuth-backed service can be injected at all (API keys now measured working).
end legend
@enduml
